          <caption>
            <p>Cutaneous Horn or Cornu Cutaneum. A cutaneous horn is characterized by compacted keratin that projects outward, resembling a pillar or an animal's horn, which gives the lesion its name. Contributed by BCK Patel, MD, FRCS</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Cutaneous__horn2" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-26600.s17">
        <fig id="article-26600.image.f2" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Skin Tag Appearance. Acrochordons vary in appearance and may be skin-colored or pigmented, often presenting as dome-shaped or papillomatous elevations. In contrast, common warts, caused by human papillomavirus, are small skin growths that can be white, beige, or brown and appear on various parts of the body. Contributed by BCK Patel, MD, FRCS</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Acrochordon" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-26600.s18">
        <fig id="article-26600.image.f3" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Viral Papilloma. Viral papillomas, caused by human papillomavirus, are usually skin-colored and present as pedunculated, irregular growths that may shed. Contributed by BCK Patel, MD, FRCS</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Viral__papilloma" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
